      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To determine an appropriate duration for a short course of oral steroids in cases of asthma exacerbations."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "A 1,000 bed city hospital in Kobe, Japan."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "Patients with asthma exacerbations who needed hospital admission."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Following an initial treatment with a 3-day course of intravenous methylprednisolone, patients were allocated to either a 1-week (1 W) or a 2-week (2 W) course of oral prednisolone (PSL, 0.5 mg/kg)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "Peak expiratory flow rate (PEF) and rate of unscheduled hospital visits and readmission."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Twenty patients were enrolled (10 in 1 W, 10 in 2 W). Mean PEF just before starting oral PSL in 1 W and 2 W were 51 and 58% of each patient's best value. PEF was significantly improved and to a similar degree over the course of time in both 1 W and 2 W. The frequency of unexpected hospital visits during a 3-month period after discharge was similar (2 in 1 W and 2 in 2 W). No readmission occurred during the same period."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Because both the 1-week and the 2-week course of oral PSL were equally effective in the treatment of asthma exacerbations, 1 week may be appropriate as the maximum duration of a short rescue course of oral steroids."
        }
      ]},
